# TypeScript Type Checking

## Context
- After making any code changes
- Before committing code
- When refactoring or modifying type definitions
- When integrating new dependencies
- When unsure about type correctness

## Requirements
- Run `npm run typecheck` proactively after any code changes
- Run type checks even if you think the changes are minor
- Address all TypeScript errors and warnings before considering the task complete
- Pay special attention to:
  - Unused imports and variables (TS6133)
  - Access control issues (TS2341)
  - Type mismatches
  - Missing type definitions
- Document any intentional type assertions or `any` usage with explanatory comments
- Update type definitions when modifying interfaces or class structures
- When in doubt, run the type checker

## Examples
<example>
// Good: Proactive type checking workflow
✅ Implement game entity
✅ Run type check immediately
✅ Fix any type errors
✅ Make additional changes to fix errors
✅ Run type check again
✅ Continue until all errors are resolved

// Good: Documenting intentional type assertions
// Force type assertion due to Phaser.Scene initialization requirements
const scene = this.scene as Phaser.Scene;
</example>

<example type="invalid">
❌ Making multiple changes without running type checks
❌ Waiting for someone to request a type check
❌ Ignoring TypeScript warnings about unused variables
❌ Using `any` type without justification
❌ Leaving private/public access violations unresolved

// Bad: Ignoring type safety
const someValue: any = getData();
const result = processData(someValue);  // Type safety lost
</example>

